  ("Gettysburg College" 0.4363 0.5630 0.1881 0.2003 0.2003 0.0121 0.0030 0.3491 0.0749)
  ("A review and meta-analysis of methodological and subject variables influencing the exposure-affect" 0.2087 0.7888 0.2256 0.2355 0.2355 0.0098 0.0037 0.2988 0.0098)
  ("relationship was performed on studies of the mere exposure effect published in the 20 years following" 0.2087 0.7894 0.2386 0.2484 0.2484 0.0098 0.0031 0.3501 0.0690)
  ("Zajonc's (1968)-seminal monograph. Stimulus type, stimulus complexity, presentation sequence," 0.2087 0.7899 0.2513 0.2611 0.2611 0.0098 0.0051 0.3136 0.0492)
  ("exposure duration, stimulus recognition, age of subject, delay between exposure and ratings, and" 0.2087 0.7891 0.2639 0.2737 0.2737 0.0098 0.0049 0.2654 0.0536)
  ("maximum number of stimulus presentations all influence the magnitude of the exposure effect." 0.2092 0.7897 0.2769 0.2867 0.2867 0.0098 0.0057 0.3038 0.0609)
  ("Implications of these findings are discussed in the context of previous reviews of the literature on" 0.2092 0.7892 0.2895 0.2993 0.2993 0.0098 0.0043 0.2824 0.0745)
  ("exposure effects and with respect to prevailing theoretical models of the exposure-affect relationship." 0.2087 0.7899 0.3025 0.3123 0.3123 0.0098 0.0026 0.3261 0.0522)
  ("Modifications of the 2-factor model of exposure effects that increase the heuristic value of the model" 0.2096 0.7903 0.3151 0.3249 0.3249 0.0098 0.0034 0.3409 0.0811)
  ("are described. A possible evolutionary basis of the exposure effect is discussed." 0.2096 0.6665 0.3281 0.3379 0.3379 0.0098 0.0033 0.2915 0.0180)
  ("to increased liking for a stimulus; in short, familiarity leads to" 0.5162 0.9314 0.3793 0.3903 0.3903 0.0110 0.0047 0.3336 0.0132)
  ("liking." 0.5162 0.5584 0.3932 0.4042 0.4042 0.0110 nil 0.3078 0.0422)
  ("Empirical and correlational evidence cited by Zajonc (1968)" 0.5321 0.9315 0.4083 0.4193 0.4193 0.0110 0.0042 0.3052 0.0656)
  ("in support of this hypothesis delineated what became the proto" 0.5162 0.9315 0.4225 0.4335 0.4335 0.0110 0.0034 0.3415 0.0127)
  ("typic methodologies used by other researchers to investigate the" 0.5162 0.9317 0.4367 0.4477 0.4477 0.0110 0.0038 0.3027 0.0335)
  ("exposure-affect relationship. The basic design of most labora-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.4506 0.4616 0.4616 0.0110 0.0051 0.2576 0.1019)
  ("tory mere exposure research is derived from Zajonc's (1968)" 0.5162 0.9315 0.4648 0.4758 0.4758 0.0110 0.0065 0.2554 0.0272)
  ("Experiments 1-3, and the general approach used by later re-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.4790 0.4900 0.4900 0.0110 0.0059 0.2398 0.0844)
  ("searchers to examine the exposure-affect relationship in vivo" 0.5167 0.9318 0.4932 0.5041 0.5041 0.0110 0.0059 0.2451 0.0594)
  ("(i.e., in \"naturalistic\" studies of exposure and affect) is derived" 0.5162 0.9312 0.5073 0.5183 0.5183 0.0110 0.0044 0.2742 0.0282)
  ("from the correlational evidence cited by Zajonc. Thus, the typi-" 0.5167 0.9315 0.5215 0.5325 0.5325 0.0110 0.0036 0.3183 0.0307)
  ("cal laboratory study of exposure and affect involves presenting" 0.5167 0.9320 0.5357 0.5467 0.5467 0.0110 0.0042 0.2528 0.0191)
  ("varying numbers of exposures of neutral visual stimuli (e.g.," 0.5171 0.9313 0.5496 0.5606 0.5606 0.0110 0.0060 0.2775 0.0499)
  ("line drawings, nonsense words), after which some dependent" 0.5167 0.9312 0.5641 0.5751 0.5751 0.0110 0.0061 0.2679 0.0251)
  ("measure of affect regarding each stimulus is collected. Natural-" 0.5171 0.9319 0.5780 0.5890 0.5890 0.0110 0.0042 0.3307 0.0546)
  ("istic studies, which focus on stimuli present in the natural envi-" 0.5167 0.9315 0.5922 0.6032 0.6032 0.0110 0.0041 0.3366 0.0268)
  ("ronment (e.g., names of public figures, common items), obtain" 0.5167 0.9313 0.6064 0.6174 0.6174 0.0110 0.0042 0.3827 0.0570)
  ("subjects' affect ratings of these stimuli using procedures similar" 0.5171 0.9320 0.6206 0.6315 0.6315 0.0110 0.0042 0.3288 0.0553)
  ("to those used in laboratory mere exposure studies but with fre-" 0.5167 0.9323 0.6347 0.6457 0.6457 0.0110 0.0045 0.3086 0.0132)
  ("quency of exposure estimated via some unobtrusive index such" 0.5167 0.9309 0.6489 0.6599 0.6599 0.0110 0.0036 0.3074 0.0476)
  ("as the Thorndike and Lorge (1944) word frequency count or" 0.5167 0.9316 0.6631 0.6741 0.6741 0.0110 0.0057 0.2120 0.0140)
  ("subjects' self-reports. Both laboratory and naturalistic studies" 0.5171 0.9323 0.6773 0.6883 0.6883 0.0110 0.0065 0.2597 0.0556)
  ("of the exposure effect generally utilize within-subjects designs," 0.5171 0.9317 0.6915 0.7025 0.7025 0.0110 0.0046 0.2721 0.0134)
  ("with each subject providing ratings of a number of stimuli that" 0.5167 0.9312 0.7057 0.7167 0.7167 0.0110 0.0041 0.3483 0.0297)
  ("differ in amount of previous exposure or familiarity." 0.5171 0.8604 0.7199 0.7309 0.7309 0.0110 0.0036 0.3489 0.0330)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) initial correlational evidence indicated that" 0.5325 0.9312 0.7347 0.7457 0.7457 0.0110 0.0057 0.2559 0.0551)
  ("relationships exist between the Thorndike and Lorge (1944)" 0.5171 0.9319 0.7489 0.7599 0.7599 0.0110 0.0070 0.2304 0.0839)
  ("word frequency count and the evaluative meaning of a word, as" 0.5167 0.9314 0.7631 0.7741 0.7741 0.0110 0.0037 0.3399 0.0333)
  ("well as between word frequency and attitudes toward the object" 0.5167 0.9312 0.7773 0.7883 0.7883 0.0110 0.0034 0.3343 0.0266)
  ("described by a word. Presumably, these relationships reflect at-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.7915 0.8024 0.8024 0.0110 0.0041 0.3029 0.0613)
  ("titude enhancement resulting from repeated exposure to partic-" 0.5162 0.9320 0.8056 0.8166 0.8166 0.0110 0.0034 0.3109 0.0381)
  ("ular words. Because these data are correlational, though, it is" 0.5171 0.9323 0.8198 0.8308 0.8308 0.0110 0.0056 0.2598 0.0261)
  ("impossible to demonstrate any causal relationship between" 0.5171 0.9313 0.8337 0.8447 0.8447 0.0110 0.0085 0.2825 0.0707)
  ("word frequency and evaluative meaning or attitude change." 0.5167 0.9317 0.8479 0.8589 0.8589 0.0110 0.0080 0.2335 0.0333)
  ("However, Zajonc also presented a series of experiments testing" 0.5171 0.9316 0.8624 0.8734 0.8734 0.0110 0.0046 0.3013 0.0596)
  ("his mere exposure hypothesis. Stronger evidence for the expo-" 0.5171 0.9324 0.8766 0.8876 0.8876 0.0110 0.0050 0.2521 0.0189)
  ("sure-affect relationship comes from these studies." 0.5167 0.8433 0.8908 0.9018 0.9018 0.0110 0.0048 0.3066 0.0699)
  ("Psychologists have long observed that repeated, unreinforced" 0.0850 0.4841 0.3796 0.3906 0.3906 0.0110 0.0033 0.2712 0.0875)
  ("exposure results in an increase in positive affect toward a stimu-" 0.0696 0.4848 0.3935 0.4045 0.4045 0.0110 0.0035 0.3383 0.0605)
  ("lus (Fechner, 1876; Maslow, 1937). A number of early studies" 0.0696 0.4856 0.4074 0.4184 0.4184 0.0110 0.0050 0.3242 0.0189)
  ("in this area (e.g., Meyer, 1903; Mpore & Gilliland, 1924; Wash-" 0.0696 0.4853 0.4216 0.4326 0.4326 0.0110 0.0038 0.4321 0.0126)
  ("burn, Child, & Abel, 1927) investigated the effects of repeated" 0.0696 0.4849 0.4358 0.4468 0.4468 0.0110 0.0046 0.3128 0.0367)
  ("exposures on affect ratings of music, generally finding more" 0.0700 0.4855 0.4497 0.4606 0.4606 0.0110 0.0069 0.2662 0.0653)
  ("positive ratings with increasing familiarity. Many of these ex-" 0.0696 0.4852 0.4635 0.4745 0.4745 0.0110 0.0045 0.3477 0.0507)
  ("periments were methodologically unsound, however (i.e., in-" 0.0696 0.4853 0.4774 0.4884 0.4884 0.0110 0.0078 0.3082 0.0654)
  ("volved improper control conditions or groups or no controls at" 0.0700 0.4850 0.4916 0.5026 0.5026 0.0110 0.0046 0.2758 0.0433)
  ("all), and conclusions based on their results are somewhat tenu-" 0.0696 0.4848 0.5055 0.5165 0.5165 0.0110 0.0043 0.2976 0.0257)
  ("ous. Additional indirect evidence for the hypothesis that in-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.5197 0.5307 0.5307 0.0110 0.0070 0.2413 0.0269)
  ("creased exposure leads to more positive affect toward a stimulus" 0.0700 0.4860 0.5339 0.5449 0.5449 0.0110 0.0033 0.3561 0.0478)
  ("came from studies of social interaction (e.g., Festinger, 1951;" 0.0700 0.4861 0.5474 0.5584 0.5584 0.0110 0.0067 0.2807 0.0355)
  ("Homans, 1961;Newcomb, 1963). Although these investigations" 0.0704 0.4856 0.5616 0.5726 0.5726 0.0110 0.0037 0.3547 0.0596)
  ("consistently demonstrated that interaction enhances the atti-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.5758 0.5868 0.5868 0.0110 0.0077 0.2579 0.0767)
  ("tudes of participants toward each other, it is impossible to tell" 0.0696 0.4849 0.5897 0.6007 0.6007 0.0110 0.0049 0.3039 0.0351)
  ("to what degree this is due to mere exposure, to reinforcement" 0.0700 0.4854 0.6039 0.6149 0.6149 0.0110 0.0046 0.3153 0.0134)
  ("derived from social interaction, or to some combination of these" 0.0704 0.4851 0.6178 0.6288 0.6288 0.0110 0.0034 0.3461 0.0479)
  ("(and other) variables." 0.0704 0.2104 0.6320 0.6430 0.6430 0.0110 0.0046 0.2442 0.0295)
  ("With the publication of Zajonc's (1968) monograph on the" 0.0858 0.4855 0.6459 0.6568 0.6568 0.0110 0.0056 0.3131 0.0334)
  ("\"Attitudinal Effects of Mere Exposure,\" the exposure effect be-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.6597 0.6707 0.6707 0.0110 0.0046 0.3204 0.0820)
  ("came a topic of investigation in mainstream psychology. Zajonc" 0.0704 0.4895 0.6739 0.6849 0.6849 0.0110 0.0035 0.3537 0.0348)
  ("(1968) defined the exposure effect as the observation that \"mere" 0.0704 0.4855 0.6878 0.6988 0.6988 0.0110 0.0036 0.3058 0.0436)
  ("repeated exposure of the individual to a stimulus is a sufficient" 0.0708 0.4854 0.7020 0.7130 0.7130 0.0110 0.0046 0.3031 0.0552)
  ("condition for the enhancement of his attitude toward it. By" 0.0708 0.4855 0.7162 0.7272 0.7272 0.0110 0.0065 0.2809 0.0622)
  ("'mere exposure' is meant a condition which just makes the given" 0.0704 0.4847 0.7301 0.7411 0.7411 0.0110 0.0026 0.4233 0.0380)
  ("stimulus accessible to the individual's perception\" (p. 1). Za-" 0.0704 0.4857 0.7443 0.7552 0.7552 0.0110 0.0062 0.2890 0.0573)
  ("jonc thus suggested that simple, unreinforced exposure leads" 0.0688 0.4860 0.7581 0.7691 0.7691 0.0110 0.0060 0.2452 0.0294)
  ("I would like to thank Nancy E. Cantor, Paul R. D'Agostino, Mary A." 0.0842 0.4852 0.8022 0.8120 0.8120 0.0098 0.0032 0.4426 0.0049)
  ("Languirand, Thane S. Pittman and two anonymous reviewers for their" 0.0708 0.4836 0.8149 0.8247 0.8247 0.0098 0.0035 0.3440 0.0716)
  ("helpful comments on earlier versions of the manuscript. I would also" 0.0708 0.4847 0.8278 0.8376 0.8376 0.0098 0.0047 0.3205 0.0393)
  ("like to thank Amy R. Kale and Barbara J. Hardy for help in conducting" 0.0708 0.4853 0.8405 0.8503 0.8503 0.0098 0.0029 0.3939 0.0208)
  ("literature searches for the meta-analysis." 0.0704 0.3044 0.8531 0.8629 0.8629 0.0098 0.0032 0.2953 0.0546)
  ("Correspondence concerning this article should be addressed to Rob-" 0.0842 0.4852 0.8658 0.8756 0.8756 0.0098 0.0037 0.2555 0.0935)
  ("ert F. Bornstein, Department of Psychology, Gettysburg College, Get-" 0.0704 0.4852 0.8784 0.8882 0.8882 0.0098 0.0044 0.3286 0.0151)
  ("tysburg, Pennsylvania 17325." 0.0704 0.2466 0.8910 0.9009 0.9009 0.0098 0.0046 0.2297 0.0474)
