  ("266" 0.0675 0.0949 0.0589 0.0722 0.0722 0.0133 nil 0.0001 0.0274)
  ("ROBERT F. BORNSTEIN" 0.4154 0.5794 0.0615 0.0714 0.0714 0.0098 0.0071 0.2235 0.0567)
  ("In Zajonc's (1968) first experiment, subjects were presented" 0.0829 0.4812 0.0890 0.1000 0.1000 0.0110 0.0045 0.3046 0.0137)
  ("with a series of nonsense words at frequencies ranging from 0" 0.0675 0.4819 0.1032 0.1142 0.1142 0.0110 0.0048 0.2896 0.0297)
  ("to 25 exposures; they then rated each stimulus word for \"good-" 0.0679 0.4824 0.1171 0.1281 0.1281 0.0110 0.0041 0.3225 0.0130)
  ("ness of meaning\" (i.e., the extent to which the word connotes" 0.0683 0.4823 0.1313 0.1423 0.1423 0.0110 0.0048 0.3144 0.0277)
  ("\"good\" vs. \"bad\" affect) on a seven-point scale. Zajonc found" 0.0679 0.4812 0.1452 0.1562 0.1562 0.0110 0.0045 0.2444 0.0500)
  ("a positive relationship between number of exposures and the" 0.0675 0.4819 0.1591 0.1701 0.1701 0.0110 0.0060 0.2649 0.0078)
  ("average goodness rating for a word. He then replicated this" 0.0675 0.4823 0.1733 0.1842 0.1842 0.0110 0.0068 0.2442 0.0488)
  ("study using a similar procedure but different stimuli: Chinese" 0.0675 0.4818 0.1874 0.1984 0.1984 0.0110 0.0055 0.3040 0.0359)
  ("ideographs were substituted for the nonsense words used in Ex-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.2016 0.2126 0.2126 0.0110 0.0039 0.3119 0.0716)
  ("periment 1. The results of Experiment 2 were consistent with" 0.0675 0.4809 0.2155 0.2265 0.2265 0.0110 0.0049 0.3319 0.0601)
  ("the findings of Zajonc's first study; again, rated goodness of" 0.0675 0.4820 0.2297 0.2407 0.2407 0.0110 0.0062 0.2330 0.0203)
  ("meaning was positively related to frequency of exposure." 0.0675 0.4383 0.2436 0.2546 0.2546 0.0110 0.0041 0.3196 0.0574)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) third experiment investigated the extent to" 0.0829 0.4818 0.2578 0.2688 0.2688 0.0110 0.0058 0.2770 0.0551)
  ("which typical exposure effects could be obtained with socially" 0.0671 0.4814 0.2717 0.2827 0.2827 0.0110 0.0049 0.2837 0.0401)
  ("relevant stimuli. In this experiment, subjects were shown a se-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.2858 0.2968 0.2968 0.0110 0.0039 0.3481 0.0525)
  ("ries of faces (photographs of students taken from a college year-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.2997 0.3107 0.3107 0.0110 0.0035 0.2970 0.0236)
  ("book) at different exposure frequencies, after which they were" 0.0675 0.4817 0.3139 0.3249 0.3249 0.0110 0.0052 0.2810 0.0391)
  ("asked to make liking ratings of each stimulus person on a seven-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.3278 0.3388 0.3388 0.0110 0.0031 0.3766 0.0366)
  ("point scale. A significant, positive relationship between fre-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.3420 0.3530 0.3530 0.0110 0.0073 0.2791 0.0345)
  ("quency of exposure (25 presentations was the ceiling in this" 0.0675 0.4823 0.3559 0.3669 0.3669 0.0110 0.0065 0.2381 0.0480)
  ("study) and mean liking rating of a stimulus was found." 0.0675 0.4228 0.3701 0.3811 0.3811 0.0110 0.0031 0.3827 0.0411)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) fourth study was not concerned with the rela-" 0.0829 0.4819 0.3843 0.3952 0.3952 0.0110 0.0036 0.3521 0.0555)
  ("tionship between frequency of exposure and reported affect. In-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.3981 0.4091 0.4091 0.0110 0.0034 0.2952 0.0541)
  ("stead, Experiment 4 tested the hypothesis that repeated presen-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.4123 0.4233 0.4233 0.0110 0.0045 0.3005 0.0369)
  ("tations of a word will lead to a decrease in galvanic skin re-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.4262 0.4372 0.4372 0.0110 0.0060 0.2688 0.0438)
  ("sponse (GSR) fluctuations that result from stimulus exposures." 0.0679 0.4812 0.4404 0.4514 0.4514 0.0110 0.0038 0.3349 0.0437)
  ("Fifteen nonsense words served as stimuli and were presented" 0.0675 0.4808 0.4546 0.4656 0.4656 0.0110 0.0059 0.2684 0.0448)
  ("from 1 to 25 times each in random order. Zajonc found a nega-" 0.0679 0.4819 0.4685 0.4795 0.4795 0.0110 0.0041 0.3458 0.0303)
  ("tive relationship between exposure frequency and mean GSR" 0.0671 0.4807 0.4827 0.4937 0.4937 0.0110 0.0060 0.2585 0.0234)
  ("change in response to the final stimulus presentation, suggesting" 0.0675 0.4816 0.4965 0.5075 0.5075 0.0110 0.0030 0.3255 0.0459)
  ("that repeated, unreinforced exposure to words results in habit-" 0.0671 0.4815 0.5104 0.5214 0.5214 0.0110 0.0043 0.2739 0.0259)
  ("uation and in decreased autonomic arousal following later stim-" 0.0679 0.4815 0.5246 0.5356 0.5356 0.0110 0.0037 0.3383 0.0418)
  ("ulus presentations." 0.0675 0.1908 0.5388 0.5498 0.5498 0.0110 0.0045 0.2371 0.0285)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) monograph stimulated a great deal of inter-" 0.0829 0.4815 0.5527 0.5637 0.5637 0.0110 0.0047 0.3082 0.0551)
  ("est in mere exposure effects, and in the 20 years following the" 0.0671 0.4813 0.5666 0.5776 0.5776 0.0110 0.0053 0.2828 0.0182)
  ("publication of his seminal paper, over 130 articles reporting" 0.0675 0.4812 0.5808 0.5917 0.5917 0.0110 0.0067 0.2531 0.0737)
  ("more than 200 separate experiments assessing the exposure-" 0.0679 0.4820 0.5946 0.6056 0.6056 0.0110 0.0055 0.2558 0.0347)
  ("affect relationship appeared in the psychological literature. Re-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6088 0.6198 0.6198 0.0110 0.0045 0.2639 0.0335)
  ("searchers investigating a wide range of topics—including adver-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6227 0.6337 0.6337 0.0110 0.0030 0.3667 0.0605)
  ("tising effects (Sawyer, 1981), children's reactions to novel stim-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.6369 0.6479 0.6479 0.0110 0.0048 0.3129 0.0362)
  ("uli (Hutt, 1975), subliminal influences on behavior (Bornstein," 0.0679 0.4813 0.6508 0.6618 0.6618 0.0110 0.0042 0.3194 0.0166)
  ("Leone, & Galley, 1987), social perceptions (Saegert, Swap, &" 0.0675 0.4809 0.6653 0.6763 0.6763 0.0110 0.0063 0.3019 0.0450)
  ("Zajonc, 1973), food preferences (Pliner, 1982), environmental" 0.0675 0.4808 0.6789 0.6898 0.6898 0.0110 0.0050 0.2672 0.0492)
  ("preferences (Herzog, Kaplan, & Kaplan, 1976), aesthetic judg-" 0.0675 0.4815 0.6930 0.7040 0.7040 0.0110 0.0042 0.3247 0.0745)
  ("ments (Berlyne, 1974), and verbal learning (Grush, 1976)—" 0.0679 0.4807 0.7072 0.7182 0.7182 0.0110 0.0065 0.2646 0.0402)
  ("have all used ideas and procedures from exposure effects re-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.7214 0.7324 0.7324 0.0110 0.0064 0.2053 0.0296)
  ("search." 0.0675 0.1132 0.7353 0.7463 0.7463 0.0110 nil 0.1331 0.0457)
  ("Stang (1974b) reviewed selected methodological factors in-" 0.0829 0.4811 0.7495 0.7605 0.7605 0.0110 0.0056 0.2715 0.0366)
  ("fluencing the mere exposure effect in studies reported through" 0.0679 0.4809 0.7634 0.7744 0.7744 0.0110 0.0048 0.2697 0.0596)
  ("the early 1970s, and he concluded that a heterogeneous expo-" 0.0671 0.4811 0.7773 0.7883 0.7883 0.0110 0.0054 0.2049 0.0204)
  ("sure sequence and a delay between presentations and ratings" 0.0675 0.4814 0.7911 0.8021 0.8021 0.0110 0.0058 0.2154 0.0280)
  ("enhanced the effect. In addition, Stang found that auditory," 0.0671 0.4809 0.8053 0.8163 0.8163 0.0110 0.0077 0.2305 0.0629)
  ("painting, and pattern (i.e., matrice or line drawing) stimuli pro-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.8192 0.8302 0.8302 0.0110 0.0033 0.3936 0.0588)
  ("duced weaker exposure effects than did other types of stimuli." 0.0675 0.4812 0.8331 0.8441 0.8441 0.0110 0.0050 0.2815 0.0395)
  ("Harrison (1977) also reviewed selected areas of mere exposure" 0.0675 0.4809 0.8473 0.8583 0.8583 0.0110 0.0044 0.3048 0.0586)
  ("theory and research, concluding that stimulus complexity, a" 0.0675 0.4809 0.8612 0.8722 0.8722 0.0110 0.0066 0.2745 0.0427)
  ("heterogeneous presentation sequence, and delay between expo-" 0.0675 0.4811 0.8747 0.8857 0.8857 0.0110 0.0045 0.2363 0.0939)
  ("sures and ratings all enhanced the exposure-affect relationship." 0.0675 0.4812 0.8889 0.8999 0.8999 0.0110 0.0033 0.2671 0.0341)
  ("Unfortunately, neither Stang nor Harrison assessed the influ-" 0.5133 0.9277 0.0897 0.1006 0.1006 0.0110 0.0060 0.2586 0.0960)
  ("ence of several important methodological variables (e.g., expo-" 0.5133 0.9278 0.1035 0.1145 0.1145 0.0110 0.0041 0.3031 0.0297)
  ("sure duration, maximum number of exposures) on the strength" 0.5133 0.9272 0.1174 0.1284 0.1284 0.0110 0.0035 0.3685 0.0278)
  ("of the exposure effect. Neither review assessed the influence of" 0.5129 0.9278 0.1316 0.1426 0.1426 0.0110 0.0050 0.2823 0.0135)
  ("subject variables (e.g., age of subject, personality and individual" 0.5133 0.9270 0.1455 0.1565 0.1565 0.0110 0.0033 0.3058 0.0455)
  ("difference variables) on the exposure-affect relationship. Fur-" 0.5125 0.9273 0.1597 0.1707 0.1707 0.0110 0.0057 0.2267 0.0634)
  ("thermore, neither review systematically assessed the influence" 0.5129 0.9275 0.1736 0.1846 0.1846 0.0110 0.0068 0.2926 0.0644)
  ("of different parameters." 0.5129 0.6675 0.1874 0.1984 0.1984 0.0110 0.0059 0.3076 0.0135)
  ("A number of models have been proposed to account for the" 0.5283 0.9273 0.2016 0.2126 0.2126 0.0110 0.0052 0.2785 0.0111)
  ("production of mere exposure effects, and reviews of empirical" 0.5125 0.9270 0.2155 0.2265 0.2265 0.0110 0.0048 0.3058 0.0719)
  ("support for these theoretical models by Harrison (1977) and" 0.5129 0.9266 0.2297 0.2407 0.2407 0.0110 0.0066 0.2499 0.0505)
  ("Stang (1974b) produced inconsistent and inconclusive results." 0.5129 0.9267 0.2439 0.2549 0.2549 0.0110 0.0049 0.2344 0.0366)
  ("In part, these inconsistencies were due to unsystematic sam-" 0.5129 0.9278 0.2578 0.2688 0.2688 0.0110 0.0062 0.2847 0.0138)
  ("pling of the mere exposure literature. Because certain critical" 0.5125 0.9266 0.2720 0.2830 0.2830 0.0110 0.0053 0.2709 0.0337)
  ("methodological and subject variables were not assessed in these" 0.5129 0.9272 0.2862 0.2971 0.2971 0.0110 0.0043 0.2976 0.0994)
  ("reviews, predictions made by different theoretical models have" 0.5125 0.9272 0.3000 0.3110 0.3110 0.0110 0.0041 0.3100 0.0534)
  ("not been adequately tested against each other. Thus, no single" 0.5133 0.9276 0.3139 0.3249 0.3249 0.0110 0.0052 0.2460 0.0213)
  ("model of exposure effects has clearly emerged as one that is best" 0.5129 0.9270 0.3281 0.3391 0.3391 0.0110 0.0033 0.3389 0.0412)
  ("supported by the range of empirical findings in this area (see" 0.5129 0.9272 0.3420 0.3530 0.3530 0.0110 0.0048 0.2645 0.0643)
  ("Harrison, 1977; Sawyer, 1981; Vanbeseleare, 1983). Many key" 0.5133 0.9268 0.3562 0.3672 0.3672 0.0110 0.0039 0.3292 0.0619)
  ("variables influencing the exposure effect are best assessed using" 0.5129 0.9274 0.3701 0.3811 0.3811 0.0110 0.0041 0.2505 0.0588)
  ("meta-analytic techniques (Rosenthal, 1984). Meta-analysis per-" 0.5133 0.9273 0.3843 0.3952 0.3952 0.0110 0.0032 0.3369 0.0901)
  ("mits a systematic assessment of the influence of each critical" 0.5129 0.9270 0.3981 0.4091 0.4091 0.0110 0.0056 0.2920 0.0294)
  ("variable on the size of the exposure effect and is therefore the" 0.5129 0.9272 0.4123 0.4233 0.4233 0.0110 0.0049 0.2365 0.0529)
  ("most appropriate technique for assessing the fit of different the-" 0.5129 0.9273 0.4265 0.4375 0.4375 0.0110 0.0035 0.3032 0.0323)
  ("oretical models of the exposure effect with the results of rele-" 0.5129 0.9278 0.4404 0.4514 0.4514 0.0110 0.0053 0.2825 0.0481)
  ("vant empirical studies." 0.5129 0.6621 0.4543 0.4653 0.4653 0.0110 0.0062 0.3155 0.0279)
  ("Advantages, limitations, and potential problems in the use of" 0.5279 0.9270 0.4685 0.4795 0.4795 0.0110 0.0038 0.3498 0.0792)
  ("meta-analysis as a technique for assessing the empirical litera-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.4824 0.4933 0.4933 0.0110 0.0048 0.3030 0.0906)
  ("ture on a topic have been discussed elsewhere (Booth-Kewley" 0.5125 0.9263 0.4962 0.5072 0.5072 0.0110 0.0056 0.2693 0.0272)
  ("& Friedman, 1987; Light & Kllemer, 1984; Rosenthal, 1984)." 0.5125 0.9272 0.5104 0.5214 0.5214 0.0110 0.0049 0.3463 0.0128)
  ("Generally, criticisms of meta-analysis fall into four areas: (a)" 0.5125 0.9274 0.5246 0.5356 0.5356 0.0110 0.0065 0.2984 0.0674)
  ("sampling bias with respect to experiments included in the meta-" 0.5125 0.9265 0.5385 0.5495 0.5495 0.0110 0.0031 0.3905 0.0608)
  ("analysis (including failure to sample unpublished, nonsignifi-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.5527 0.5637 0.5637 0.0110 0.0062 0.2724 0.0523)
  ("cant findings); (b) variability in the strength and methodological" 0.5121 0.9262 0.5666 0.5776 0.5776 0.0110 0.0031 0.3408 0.0278)
  ("soundness of different experiments; (c) averaging effect sizes" 0.5125 0.9273 0.5808 0.5917 0.5917 0.0110 0.0072 0.2437 0.0674)
  ("across studies that vary markedly with respect to methodolo-" 0.5125 0.9274 0.5946 0.6056 0.6056 0.0110 0.0052 0.3002 0.0415)
  ("gies, variables assessed, characteristics of subjects, selection" 0.5121 0.9259 0.6088 0.6198 0.6198 0.0110 0.0096 0.2468 0.0288)
  ("procedures, and so on (i.e., combining \"apples and oranges\";" 0.5125 0.9274 0.6227 0.6337 0.6337 0.0110 0.0055 0.2532 0.0769)
  ("see Glass, 1978); and (d) attempting to summarize a broad," 0.5125 0.9267 0.6366 0.6476 0.6476 0.0110 0.0063 0.3009 0.0214)
  ("complex issue or topic with a few overall effect size estimates." 0.5125 0.9262 0.6508 0.6618 0.6618 0.0110 0.0051 0.3320 0.0565)
  ("The nature of the exposure effects literature and the questions" 0.5125 0.9269 0.6650 0.6760 0.6760 0.0110 0.0047 0.2491 0.0261)
  ("being addressed in this review are such that meta-analysis of" 0.5125 0.9266 0.6789 0.6898 0.6898 0.0110 0.0057 0.2669 0.0362)
  ("research on the exposure-affect relationship has many advan-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.6930 0.7040 0.7040 0.0110 0.0049 0.2523 0.0539)
  ("tages and none of these disadvantages." 0.5121 0.7621 0.7072 0.7182 0.7182 0.0110 0.0033 0.2597 0.0330)
  ("Meta-analytic techniques are particularly suited to an assess-" 0.5275 0.9265 0.7211 0.7321 0.7321 0.0110 0.0038 0.3213 0.0918)
  ("ment of the exposure effects literature for several reasons. First," 0.5125 0.9263 0.7350 0.7460 0.7460 0.0110 0.0040 0.2897 0.0337)
  ("the majority of studies conducted in this area after the publica-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.7492 0.7602 0.7602 0.0110 0.0040 0.3111 0.0204)
  ("tion of Zajonc's (1968) monograph used very similar methodol-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.7631 0.7741 0.7741 0.0110 0.0035 0.3555 0.0260)
  ("ogies (derived from Zajonc's methods and procedures), so that" 0.5121 0.9258 0.7770 0.7879 0.7879 0.0110 0.0047 0.2885 0.0332)
  ("comparison of results across studies is not unduly influenced" 0.5121 0.9262 0.7908 0.8018 0.8018 0.0110 0.0060 0.2483 0.0763)
  ("by researchers' using a wide variety of methodologies to assess" 0.5121 0.9269 0.8047 0.8157 0.8157 0.0110 0.0042 0.3038 0.0162)
  ("the exposure-affect relationship (Criticisms b and c, noted ear-" 0.5121 0.9269 0.8189 0.8299 0.8299 0.0110 0.0037 0.3090 0.0204)
  ("lier). The limited range of dependent measures, stimuli, and ex-" 0.5125 0.9269 0.8328 0.8438 0.8438 0.0110 0.0035 0.3649 0.0304)
  ("posure paradigms used in these experiments help to reduce be-" 0.5121 0.9265 0.8467 0.8577 0.8577 0.0110 0.0040 0.3048 0.0447)
  ("tween-studies variance (Criticism c). Because methodological" 0.5125 0.9262 0.8606 0.8715 0.8715 0.0110 0.0057 0.3050 0.0893)
  ("and subject variables can be classified into discrete categories," 0.5121 0.9267 0.8744 0.8854 0.8854 0.0110 0.0046 0.2497 0.0239)
  ("and because separate effect size estimates can be calculated" 0.5125 0.9266 0.8883 0.8993 0.8993 0.0110 0.0080 0.2274 0.0239))
  (3
  ("EXPOSURE AND AFFECT" 0.4113 0.5908 0.0612 0.0711 0.0711 0.0098 0.0064 0.1608 0.0779)
  ("within each category of a particular variable, the influence of" 0.0696 0.4869 0.0884 0.0994 0.0994 0.0110 0.0053 0.2538 0.0430)
  ("specific variables on the exposure-affect relationship can be as-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.1029 0.1139 0.1139 0.0110 0.0040 0.2664 0.0481)
  ("sessed (Criticism d). Many critical variables influencing the ex-" 0.0700 0.4857 0.1168 0.1278 0.1278 0.0110 0.0041 0.3472 0.0390)
  ("posure effect (e.g., age of subject, exposure duration, maximum" 0.0704 0.4855 0.1310 0.1420 0.1420 0.0110 0.0038 0.3516 0.0441)
  ("number of stimulus exposures) typically vary between, rather" 0.0704 0.4849 0.1449 0.1559 0.1559 0.0110 0.0052 0.2968 0.0523)
  ("than within, studies, so that systematic assessments of these" 0.0700 0.4859 0.1588 0.1697 0.1697 0.0110 0.0070 0.2994 0.0298)
  ("variables can only be performed by combining the results of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.1729 0.1839 0.1839 0.0110 0.0059 0.2684 0.0586)
  ("different experiments via meta-analytic techniques. For many" 0.0700 0.4851 0.1868 0.1978 0.1978 0.0110 0.0051 0.3163 0.0541)
  ("variables (e.g., exposure duration, delay between exposures and" 0.0704 0.4849 0.2010 0.2120 0.2120 0.0110 0.0035 0.2932 0.0586)
  ("ratings), a traditional comparison of the results of individual" 0.0708 0.4853 0.2149 0.2259 0.2259 0.0110 0.0062 0.2689 0.0537)
  ("studies produces conflicting results, whereas meta-analysis of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.2291 0.2401 0.2401 0.0110 0.0055 0.2759 0.0456)
  ("all studies classified into salient dimensions for the variable in" 0.0704 0.4855 0.2433 0.2543 0.2543 0.0110 0.0048 0.2939 0.0165)
  ("question produces clearer and more consistent findings (see" 0.0704 0.4863 0.2575 0.2685 0.2685 0.0110 0.0072 0.2304 0.0552)
  ("Light & Pillemer [1984], Rosenthal [1984], and Masters, Can-" 0.0712 0.4861 0.2710 0.2820 0.2820 0.0110 0.0049 0.3571 0.0354)
  ("tor, & Hedges [1988] for discussions of the value of meta-ana-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.2852 0.2962 0.2962 0.0110 0.0041 0.3167 0.0221)
  ("lytic techniques in such circumstances). Finally, a fail-safe N" 0.0708 0.4859 0.2994 0.3104 0.3104 0.0110 0.0060 0.2724 0.0289)
  ("(Rosenthal, 1979, 1984) can be calculated for each effect size" 0.0704 0.4859 0.3133 0.3243 0.3243 0.0110 0.0063 0.2313 0.0763)
  ("estimate in the meta-analysis to assess the possible influence of" 0.0704 0.4857 0.3275 0.3385 0.3385 0.0110 0.0043 0.3126 0.0551)
  ("sampling bias (i.e., underestimate of unpublished, nonsignifi-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.3414 0.3524 0.3524 0.0110 0.0065 0.3026 0.0613)
  ("cant findings) on effect sizes (Criticism a)." 0.0704 0.3454 0.3556 0.3666 0.3666 0.0110 0.0035 0.3385 0.0280)
  ("The purpose of this article is to examine systematically the" 0.0854 0.4859 0.3694 0.3804 0.3804 0.0110 0.0052 0.3217 0.0265)
  ("influence of methodological and subject variables on the expo-" 0.0704 0.4861 0.3836 0.3946 0.3946 0.0110 0.0046 0.2693 0.0605)
  ("sure-affect relationship, in order to assess support for different" 0.0708 0.4858 0.3975 0.4085 0.4085 0.0110 0.0050 0.2244 0.0699)
  ("theoretical models of the exposure effect. The analysis involves" 0.0704 0.4869 0.4117 0.4227 0.4227 0.0110 0.0043 0.3054 0.0692)
  ("several steps. First, studies of the exposure-affect relationship" 0.0708 0.4860 0.4256 0.4366 0.4366 0.0110 0.0056 0.2328 0.0445)
  ("that appeared in the first two decades following publication of" 0.0708 0.4857 0.4398 0.4508 0.4508 0.0110 0.0054 0.2782 0.0259)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) monograph are reviewed. The influence of each" 0.0708 0.4892 0.4537 0.4647 0.4647 0.0110 0.0035 0.3288 0.0560)
  ("methodological and subject variable on the exposure effect is" 0.0708 0.4864 0.4679 0.4788 0.4788 0.0110 0.0062 0.2452 0.1003)
  ("assessed using meta-analytic techniques (Rosenthal, 1984). For" 0.0708 0.4857 0.4820 0.4930 0.4930 0.0110 0.0047 0.2992 0.0518)
  ("reasons that are discussed later, certain variables (e.g., stimulus" 0.0712 0.4864 0.4959 0.5069 0.5069 0.0110 0.0044 0.3026 0.0485)
  ("complexity) could not be subjected to meta-analysis. However," 0.0712 0.4862 0.5098 0.5208 0.5208 0.0110 0.0048 0.3120 0.0795)
  ("these variables are discussed in the following sections as they" 0.0708 0.4855 0.5237 0.5347 0.5347 0.0110 0.0060 0.2427 0.0337)
  ("apply to particular issues. The implications of this review and" 0.0708 0.4854 0.5379 0.5489 0.5489 0.0110 0.0049 0.3059 0.0367)
  ("analysis are discussed in the context of theoretical models of" 0.0712 0.4865 0.5521 0.5631 0.5631 0.0110 0.0061 0.2484 0.0523)
  ("mere exposure effects. Empirical support for these models is" 0.0712 0.4864 0.5663 0.5773 0.5773 0.0110 0.0059 0.2960 0.0334)
  ("assessed, and modifications in the best-supported model of ex-" 0.0712 0.4865 0.5801 0.5911 0.5911 0.0110 0.0045 0.3067 0.0580)
  ("posure effects are described, which allow the model to better" 0.0712 0.4861 0.5943 0.6053 0.6053 0.0110 0.0061 0.2861 0.0448)
  ("accommodate the range of findings in this area. An evolution-" 0.0708 0.4865 0.6082 0.6192 0.6192 0.0110 0.0041 0.3305 0.0912)
  ("ary basis of the exposure effect is briefly discussed in the context" 0.0712 0.4858 0.6221 0.6331 0.6331 0.0110 0.0035 0.3003 0.0211)
  ("of findings from this review." 0.0717 0.2541 0.6363 0.6473 0.6473 0.0110 0.0038 0.3388 0.0135)
  ("Meta-Analysis" 0.2258 0.3323 0.6703 0.6824 0.6824 0.0121 nil 0.4141 0.1065)
  ("Inclusion Criteria" 0.0717 0.2026 0.6956 0.7077 0.7077 0.0121 0.0056 0.2746 0.0680)
  ("This review and meta-analysis of the exposure-affect rela-" 0.0871 0.4869 0.7205 0.7315 0.7315 0.0110 0.0062 0.2564 0.0300)
  ("tionship includes studies of unreinforced exposure and affect" 0.0712 0.4866 0.7344 0.7454 0.7454 0.0110 0.0058 0.2132 0.0546)
  ("that were published between 1968 and 1987. The review in-" 0.0712 0.4865 0.7486 0.7596 0.7596 0.0110 0.0067 0.2504 0.0262)
  ("cludes only articles published after Zajonc's (1968) monograph" 0.0717 0.4859 0.7625 0.7734 0.7734 0.0110 0.0043 0.3103 0.0419)
  ("for several reasons. First, studies in this area conducted prior to" 0.0717 0.4864 0.7766 0.7876 0.7876 0.0110 0.0043 0.2746 0.0172)
  ("the mid-1960s were often methodologically unsound (see Za-" 0.0717 0.4869 0.7908 0.8018 0.8018 0.0110 0.0053 0.2825 0.0201)
  ("jonc, 1968). Many early studies used improper control groups" 0.0700 0.4869 0.8047 0.8157 0.8157 0.0110 0.0050 0.3125 0.0332)
  ("or conditions, or no controls at all, making it impossible to de-" 0.0717 0.4869 0.8189 0.8299 0.8299 0.0110 0.0043 0.3435 0.0142)
  ("rive effect size estimates for meta-analysis. With the publication" 0.0721 0.4863 0.8325 0.8435 0.8435 0.0110 0.0035 0.3952 0.0253)
  ("of Zajonc's seminal paper and his description of experimental" 0.0712 0.4862 0.8467 0.8577 0.8577 0.0110 0.0048 0.3010 0.0139)
  ("approaches to the study of exposure effects, the methodologies" 0.0717 0.4873 0.8609 0.8719 0.8719 0.0110 0.0048 0.2707 0.0741)
  ("used in these studies have improved markedly and have become" 0.0721 0.4863 0.8744 0.8854 0.8854 0.0110 0.0033 0.3485 0.0299)
  ("much more consistent across experiments. Limiting this review" 0.0717 0.4860 0.8883 0.8993 0.8993 0.0110 0.0041 0.3649 0.0372)
  ("267" 0.9046 0.9317 0.0600 0.0710 0.0710 0.0110 nil 0.0001 0.0271)
  ("to papers published since 1968 allows for discussion of individ-" 0.5162 0.9319 0.0884 0.0994 0.0994 0.0110 0.0042 0.2954 0.0130)
  ("ual experiments; if the many pre-1968 studies were included, it" 0.5167 0.9320 0.1026 0.1136 0.1136 0.0110 0.0036 0.3461 0.0206)
  ("would simply become unwieldy owing to the large number of" 0.5167 0.9320 0.1165 0.1275 0.1275 0.0110 0.0044 0.3378 0.0404)
  ("papers that have little value concerning the theoretical and em-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.1307 0.1417 0.1417 0.0110 0.0037 0.3030 0.0429)
  ("pirical issues raised in this review. Stang (1974b) reviewed stud-" 0.5167 0.9319 0.1446 0.1556 0.1556 0.0110 0.0038 0.3322 0.0423)
  ("ies in this area that were published prior to 1968." 0.5171 0.8387 0.1588 0.1697 0.1697 0.0110 0.0035 0.3296 0.0185)
  ("This review includes only experiments that investigate affect" 0.5325 0.9321 0.1726 0.1836 0.1836 0.0110 0.0039 0.3265 0.0300)
  ("changes in response to mere (i.e., unreinforced) exposure to vi-" 0.5167 0.9324 0.1868 0.1978 0.1978 0.0110 0.0038 0.3177 0.0523)
  ("sual and auditory stimuli. The vast majority of mere exposure" 0.5171 0.9326 0.2007 0.2117 0.2117 0.0110 0.0044 0.3318 0.0264)
  ("experiments have studied attitudes toward these stimuli. Stud-" 0.5171 0.9323 0.2149 0.2259 0.2259 0.0110 0.0052 0.3274 0.0811)
  ("ies that involve exposure to other types of stimuli (i.e., olfactory" 0.5171 0.9322 0.2291 0.2401 0.2401 0.0110 0.0035 0.3620 0.0184)
  ("and gustatory) present a different set of methodological issues" 0.5171 0.9327 0.2430 0.2540 0.2540 0.0110 0.0051 0.2562 0.0240)
  ("that are only tangentially related to findings obtained using bet-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.2572 0.2682 0.2682 0.0110 0.0033 0.3238 0.0262)
  ("ter-controlled visual and auditory stimuli. Certain key variables" 0.5171 0.9327 0.2710 0.2820 0.2820 0.0110 0.0035 0.3375 0.0891)
  ("(e.g., exposure duration, stimulus complexity) are difficult to" 0.5171 0.9322 0.2849 0.2959 0.2959 0.0110 0.0056 0.3135 0.0315)
  ("measure in olfactory and gustatory stimuli, making interexperi-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.2991 0.3101 0.3101 0.0110 0.0030 0.3826 0.0558)
  ("ment comparisons difficult. In addition, exposure to certain" 0.5175 0.9313 0.3133 0.3243 0.3243 0.0110 0.0072 0.2862 0.0337)
  ("gustatory and olfactory stimuli may be reinforcing in and of" 0.5167 0.9316 0.3272 0.3382 0.3382 0.0110 0.0059 0.2805 0.0619)
  ("itself (see, e.g., C. S. Crandall's, 1984, study of exposure effects" 0.5171 0.9326 0.3414 0.3524 0.3524 0.0110 0.0038 0.3294 0.0316)
  ("with doughnuts), so that such experiments can no longer be" 0.5171 0.9322 0.3553 0.3662 0.3662 0.0110 0.0065 0.2521 0.0293)
  ("strictly classified as mere exposure research. Although these" 0.5171 0.9322 0.3694 0.3804 0.3804 0.0110 0.0063 0.2672 0.0451)
  ("studies are not included in the meta-analysis, they will be dis-" 0.5171 0.9328 0.3833 0.3943 0.3943 0.0110 0.0053 0.3091 0.0449)
  ("cussed in later sections of this article." 0.5171 0.7613 0.3975 0.4085 0.4085 0.0110 0.0039 0.2784 0.0424)
  ("Studies that include any type of reinforcement introduced" 0.5329 0.9324 0.4117 0.4227 0.4227 0.0110 0.0056 0.2520 0.0481)
  ("during the exposure or rating phases of the experiment are not" 0.5171 0.9320 0.4256 0.4366 0.4366 0.0110 0.0039 0.2958 0.0449)
  ("included in the meta-analysis, to avoid contaminating the anal-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.4398 0.4508 0.4508 0.0110 0.0038 0.3356 0.0570)
  ("ysis with experiments investigating phenomena other than un-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.4537 0.4647 0.4647 0.0110 0.0040 0.3247 0.0243)
  ("reinforced stimulus exposure. A number of experiments inves-" 0.5171 0.9323 0.4679 0.4788 0.4788 0.0110 0.0036 0.3421 0.0675)
  ("tigating familiarity effects in social settings (e.g., Ebbeson, Kjos," 0.5171 0.9325 0.4820 0.4930 0.4930 0.0110 0.0040 0.3393 0.0503)
  ("& Konecni, 1976; Segal, 1974) were eliminated on the basis of" 0.5175 0.9330 0.4959 0.5069 0.5069 0.0110 0.0041 0.3425 0.0132)
  ("this criterion, because it was not clear to what extent attitude" 0.5175 0.9326 0.5098 0.5208 0.5208 0.0110 0.0054 0.2972 0.0248)
  ("changes in these studies were induced by mere exposure or by" 0.5171 0.9322 0.5240 0.5350 0.5350 0.0110 0.0045 0.2744 0.0523)
  ("reinforcement derived from repeated social interaction." 0.5175 0.8829 0.5379 0.5489 0.5489 0.0110 0.0056 0.2859 0.0930)
  ("In addition, the numerous studies of the relationship between" 0.5333 0.9322 0.5518 0.5628 0.5628 0.0110 0.0035 0.3482 0.0135)
  ("novelty-familiarity and infant attention/looking time that fol-" 0.5175 0.9328 0.5660 0.5769 0.5769 0.0110 0.0054 0.3129 0.1260)
  ("lowed Fantz's (1964) experiment (e.g., Balogh & Porter, 1986;" 0.5175 0.9328 0.5798 0.5908 0.5908 0.0110 0.0053 0.3287 0.0379)
  ("Faw & Nunnally, 1968; D. Greenberg, Uzgiris, & Hunt, 1970;" 0.5179 0.9332 0.5943 0.6053 0.6053 0.0110 0.0042 0.3740 0.0266)
  ("Hunter, Ames, & Koopman, 1983; Hunter, Ross, & Ames," 0.5175 0.9321 0.6079 0.6189 0.6189 0.0110 0.0070 0.3409 0.0504)
  ("1982; Nachmann, Stern, & Best, 1986) are not included in the" 0.5196 0.9326 0.6221 0.6331 0.6331 0.0110 0.0047 0.3385 0.0361)
  ("meta-analysis. The relationship between attention and affect" 0.5179 0.9325 0.6363 0.6473 0.6473 0.0110 0.0083 0.2792 0.0920)
  ("may vary as a function of experimental paradigm and age of" 0.5179 0.9324 0.6502 0.6612 0.6612 0.0110 0.0059 0.2762 0.0274)
  ("subject (see D. Greenberg et al., 1970; Nachmann et al., 1986;" 0.5175 0.9332 0.6644 0.6753 0.6753 0.0110 0.0043 0.3263 0.0459)
  ("Wetherford & Cohen, 1973), and research suggests that both" 0.5179 0.9326 0.6785 0.6895 0.6895 0.0110 0.0065 0.2967 0.0738)
  ("pleasant stimuli and extremely unpleasant stimuli (e.g., depic-" 0.5179 0.9332 0.6924 0.7034 0.7034 0.0110 0.0048 0.3448 0.0539)
  ("tions of violent acts) are associated with increased attention and" 0.5175 0.9320 0.7066 0.7176 0.7176 0.0110 0.0032 0.3223 0.0322)
  ("scrutiny (Scrivner & Safer, 1988). Thus, treating visual prefer-" 0.5179 0.9327 0.7205 0.7315 0.7315 0.0110 0.0047 0.2880 0.0542)
  ("ence or fixation time as an affect measure comparable to those" 0.5179 0.9330 0.7347 0.7457 0.7457 0.0110 0.0041 0.3240 0.0301)
  ("obtained via verbal report is unwarranted. Studies of novelty-" 0.5179 0.9324 0.7486 0.7596 0.7596 0.0110 0.0046 0.2777 0.0564)
  ("familiarity and infant attention are discussed later, in the con-" 0.5179 0.9328 0.7628 0.7738 0.7738 0.0110 0.0052 0.2746 0.0694)
  ("text of findings from exposure effects research." 0.5179 0.8246 0.7766 0.7876 0.7876 0.0110 0.0038 0.2791 0.0250)
  ("Finally, the meta-analysis includes only studies that used hu-" 0.5337 0.9332 0.7908 0.8018 0.8018 0.0110 0.0042 0.3074 0.0492)
  ("man subjects. A number of investigations in this area have used" 0.5179 0.9320 0.8047 0.8157 0.8157 0.0110 0.0033 0.3625 0.0284)
  ("infrahuman subjects (e.g., Navarick & Strouthes, 1969;" 0.5179 0.9332 0.8186 0.8296 0.8296 0.0110 0.0127 0.3477 0.0773)
  ("Strouthes, 1971); these experiments were reviewed by Hill" 0.5183 0.9328 0.8328 0.8438 0.8438 0.0110 0.0083 0.2843 0.0670)
  ("(1978), who found strong support for the existence of exposure" 0.5175 0.9330 0.8467 0.8577 0.8577 0.0110 0.0037 0.2820 0.0479)
  ("effects in infrahumans. However, the methodologies, stimuli," 0.5179 0.9325 0.8606 0.8715 0.8715 0.0110 0.0083 0.3217 0.0394)
  ("and dependent measures used in these studies differ so signifi-" 0.5179 0.9335 0.8747 0.8857 0.8857 0.0110 0.0051 0.2594 0.0239)
  ("cantly from those used in exposure effects research with human" 0.5175 0.9322 0.8886 0.8996 0.8996 0.0110 0.0034 0.3462 0.0405))
  (4
  ("268" 0.0704 0.0977 0.0580 0.0713 0.0713 0.0133 nil 0.0001 0.0273)
  ("ROBERT F. BORNSTEIN" 0.4175 0.5815 0.0606 0.0704 0.0704 0.0098 0.0067 0.2449 0.0566)
  ("subjects that combining the results of human and animal stud-" 0.0700 0.4844 0.0875 0.0985 0.0985 0.0110 0.0040 0.3440 0.0523)
  ("ies for meta-analysis is unwarranted. Results from infrahuman" 0.0696 0.4834 0.1014 0.1124 0.1124 0.0110 0.0039 0.3393 0.0181)
  ("studies are discussed later." 0.0700 0.2408 0.1156 0.1266 0.1266 0.0110 0.0039 0.2492 0.0449)
  ("Procedure" 0.0696 0.1444 0.1480 0.1602 0.1602 0.0121 nil 0.1441 0.0748)
  ("An extensive literature search of Psychological Abstracts was" 0.0850 0.4843 0.1717 0.1827 0.1827 0.0110 0.0046 0.2922 0.0198)
  ("conducted to obtain references for studies of mere exposure" 0.0700 0.4843 0.1859 0.1969 0.1969 0.0110 0.0068 0.2230 0.0678)
  ("effects in human subjects published between 1968 and 1987. In" 0.0696 0.4838 0.1998 0.2108 0.2108 0.0110 0.0043 0.3000 0.0398)
  ("addition, a computer search using the PsychlNFO data base" 0.0700 0.4843 0.2140 0.2250 0.2250 0.0110 0.0076 0.2590 0.0577)
  ("was performed to obtain any published articles that had not" 0.0696 0.4833 0.2282 0.2392 0.2392 0.0110 0.0066 0.2560 0.0251)
  ("been included in Psychological Abstracts. The following key" 0.0696 0.4834 0.2420 0.2530 0.2530 0.0110 0.0069 0.2395 0.0308)
  ("words were used to search Psychological Abstracts and Psych-" 0.0696 0.4834 0.2562 0.2672 0.2672 0.0110 0.0051 0.2556 0.0402)
  ("lNFO: mere exposure, exposure effects, exposure-affect, famil-" 0.0696 0.4839 0.2701 0.2811 0.2811 0.0110 0.0064 0.3872 0.0425)
  ("iarity-affect, familiarity effects, stimulus frequency-affect, stim-" 0.0704 0.4851 0.2843 0.2953 0.2953 0.0110 0.0123 0.4459 0.0685)
  ("ulus duration-affect, stimulus complexity-affect, stimulus inter-" 0.0704 0.4839 0.2982 0.3092 0.3092 0.0110 0.0034 0.4190 0.0279)
  ("val-affect, novelty-affect, and novelty effects. All published" 0.0704 0.4829 0.3124 0.3234 0.3234 0.0110 0.0194 0.5683 0.0532)
  ("studies of the exposure-affect relationship conducted between" 0.0696 0.4830 0.3263 0.3373 0.3373 0.0110 0.0057 0.2423 0.0442)
  ("1968 and 1987 that were cited in these articles were also in-" 0.0717 0.4836 0.3405 0.3514 0.3514 0.0110 0.0059 0.2954 0.0232)
  ("cluded. The only inclusion criteria for studies (discussed in de-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.3543 0.3653 0.3653 0.0110 0.0040 0.2795 0.0480)
  ("tail earlier) were that (a) they were conducted on human sub-" 0.0692 0.4836 0.3685 0.3795 0.3795 0.0110 0.0052 0.3079 0.0209)
  ("jects; (b) they were studies of unreinforced exposure to visual" 0.0675 0.4828 0.3824 0.3934 0.3934 0.0110 0.0047 0.2617 0.0358)
  ("or auditory stimuli; and (c) they involved some dependent mea-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.3966 0.4076 0.4076 0.0110 0.0029 0.3683 0.0139)
  ("sure of affect after stimulus exposures. Overall, 134 published" 0.0692 0.4829 0.4108 0.4218 0.4218 0.0110 0.0060 0.2794 0.0281)
  ("articles reporting a total of 208 independent effect sizes were" 0.0696 0.4834 0.4250 0.4360 0.4360 0.0110 0.0057 0.2565 0.0468)
  ("included in the meta-analysis." 0.0692 0.2654 0.4389 0.4498 0.4498 0.0110 0.0038 0.3107 0.0574)
  ("Following Rosenthal (1984), I used the product-moment cor-" 0.0846 0.4832 0.4527 0.4637 0.4637 0.0110 0.0031 0.3468 0.0645)
  ("relation coefficient (r) as the effect-size estimate for meta-analy-" 0.0700 0.4840 0.4669 0.4779 0.4779 0.0110 0.0043 0.3089 0.0505)
  ("sis. The correlation coefficient r was chosen as the effect-size" 0.0696 0.4834 0.4808 0.4918 0.4918 0.0110 0.0063 0.2550 0.0196)
  ("estimate because studies of the exposure effect typically assess" 0.0696 0.4844 0.4950 0.5060 0.5060 0.0110 0.0052 0.2601 0.0553)
  ("the relationship between two continuous variables (number of" 0.0696 0.4828 0.5089 0.5199 0.5199 0.0110 0.0044 0.3175 0.0203)
  ("stimulus exposures and an affect rating of the stimulus), so that" 0.0696 0.4833 0.5231 0.5341 0.5341 0.0110 0.0036 0.3432 0.0569)
  ("r seemed the most easily comprehensible representation of the" 0.0700 0.4842 0.5370 0.5479 0.5479 0.0110 0.0048 0.3066 0.0063)
  ("magnitude of the exposure-affect relationship. Combined (av-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.5511 0.5621 0.5621 0.0110 0.0050 0.2994 0.0709)
  ("erage) effect sizes were computed by transforming each r into a" 0.0696 0.4834 0.5653 0.5763 0.5763 0.0110 0.0038 0.3363 0.0403)
  ("Fisher's z coefficient, obtaining the sum of the Fisher's zs, divid-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.5792 0.5902 0.5902 0.0110 0.0037 0.3569 0.0511)
  ("ing each sum by the number of studies used to obtain it, and" 0.0696 0.4829 0.5934 0.6044 0.6044 0.0110 0.0055 0.2981 0.0207)
  ("converting the resulting z back into an r (Rosenthal, 1984). The" 0.0696 0.4834 0.6073 0.6183 0.6183 0.0110 0.0033 0.3319 0.0703)
  ("Stouffer method of adding standard normal deviate zs (Mos-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.6215 0.6325 0.6325 0.0110 0.0060 0.3000 0.0500)
  ("teller & Bush, 1954; described by Rosenthal, 1984) was used" 0.0696 0.4829 0.6354 0.6464 0.6464 0.0110 0.0056 0.2902 0.0336)
  ("to estimate significance levels for combined studies. Indices of" 0.0692 0.4832 0.6496 0.6605 0.6605 0.0110 0.0051 0.3205 0.0129)
  ("effect size (r and z) were given a positive sign when they were in" 0.0692 0.4822 0.6637 0.6747 0.6747 0.0110 0.0032 0.3652 0.0331)
  ("the theoretically predicted direction (i.e., when affect was en-" 0.0692 0.4836 0.6776 0.6886 0.6886 0.0110 0.0063 0.2824 0.0202)
  ("hanced by repeated exposures) and were given a negative sign" 0.0696 0.4826 0.6915 0.7025 0.7025 0.0110 0.0053 0.2483 0.0472)
  ("when they were in the direction opposite to that predicted by" 0.0696 0.4830 0.7057 0.7167 0.7167 0.0110 0.0054 0.2713 0.0351)
  ("Zajonc's (1968) hypothesis (i.e., when the relationship between" 0.0692 0.4826 0.7196 0.7306 0.7306 0.0110 0.0039 0.3259 0.0556)
  ("exposure and affect was negative)." 0.0692 0.2908 0.7338 0.7448 0.7448 0.0110 0.0046 0.2796 0.0597)
  ("A fail-safe N (Rosenthal, 1979, 1984) was also computed for" 0.0846 0.4834 0.7480 0.7589 0.7589 0.0110 0.0044 0.3229 0.0116)
  ("each combined effect size. The fail-safe Nis an estimate of the" 0.0692 0.4834 0.7618 0.7728 0.7728 0.0110 0.0050 0.3190 0.0300)
  ("number of unpublished studies averaging zero effect size that" 0.0704 0.4833 0.7757 0.7867 0.7867 0.0110 0.0056 0.2603 0.0516)
  ("would have to exist in order for the probability level associated" 0.0696 0.4829 0.7899 0.8009 0.8009 0.0110 0.0045 0.2814 0.0404)
  ("with an observed effect to be rendered nonsignificant. The fail-" 0.0692 0.4835 0.8038 0.8148 0.8148 0.0110 0.0045 0.2961 0.0293)
  ("safe N thus serves as an additional estimate of the overall sig-" 0.0700 0.4836 0.8177 0.8287 0.8287 0.0110 0.0051 0.2803 0.0244)
  ("nificance of an effect size for a given sample of studies." 0.0700 0.4246 0.8316 0.8425 0.8425 0.0110 0.0038 0.3078 0.0580)
  ("Each experiment was categorized according to all of the fol-" 0.0854 0.4836 0.8457 0.8567 0.8567 0.0110 0.0045 0.3119 0.0330)
  ("lowing parameters: type of outcome measure used, type of stim-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.8593 0.8703 0.8703 0.0110 0.0030 0.3900 0.0436)
  ("ulus used, amount of delay between stimulus exposure and rat-" 0.0696 0.4836 0.8735 0.8845 0.8845 0.0110 0.0033 0.3460 0.0281)
  ("ing, exposure sequence (i.e., \"massed\" or homogeneous vs." 0.0696 0.4833 0.8874 0.8984 0.8984 0.0110 0.0075 0.2458 0.0246)
  ("\"randomized\" or heterogeneous stimulus presentations), maxi-" 0.5158 0.9294 0.0884 0.0994 0.0994 0.0110 0.0033 0.3272 0.0958)
  ("mum number of stimulus exposures, stimulus exposure dura-" 0.5154 0.9294 0.1023 0.1133 0.1133 0.0110 0.0042 0.3255 0.0338)
  ("tion, and age of subjects." 0.5150 0.6758 0.1159 0.1269 0.1269 0.0110 0.0040 0.2923 0.0301)
  ("To examine the influence of a particular variable (e.g., stimu-" 0.5304 0.9290 0.1301 0.1411 0.1411 0.0110 0.0029 0.3657 0.0171)
  ("lus type) on the exposure effect, I classified studies into catego-" 0.5150 0.9299 0.1443 0.1552 0.1552 0.0110 0.0039 0.2814 0.0194)
  ("ries representing the salient dimensions of the variable. For" 0.5154 0.9291 0.1581 0.1691 0.1691 0.0110 0.0072 0.2488 0.0236)
  ("studies that manipulated a methodological variable within a" 0.5154 0.9292 0.1723 0.1833 0.1833 0.0110 0.0067 0.2960 0.0452)
  ("single experiment (e.g., Harrison & Zajonc, 1970, varied the" 0.5154 0.9297 0.1862 0.1972 0.1972 0.0110 0.0062 0.3131 0.0376)
  ("period of delay between exposures and ratings), separate effect" 0.5150 0.9291 0.2004 0.2114 0.2114 0.0110 0.0050 0.2621 0.0417)
  ("sizes were calculated for the different experimental conditions" 0.5154 0.9302 0.2143 0.2253 0.2253 0.0110 0.0054 0.2777 0.0306)
  ("(in this case, immediate vs. delayed ratings). This occurred in" 0.5150 0.9288 0.2285 0.2395 0.2395 0.0110 0.0047 0.3128 0.0181)
  ("a total of 17 experiments. Separate effect size estimates were" 0.5150 0.9298 0.2427 0.2537 0.2537 0.0110 0.0064 0.2911 0.0076)
  ("calculated for studies within each category (i.e., separate effect" 0.5150 0.9291 0.2565 0.2675 0.2675 0.0110 0.0043 0.2807 0.0661)
  ("size estimates were determined for painting, ideograph, and" 0.5150 0.9287 0.2704 0.2814 0.2814 0.0110 0.0067 0.2903 0.0243)
  ("nonsense-word stimuli, etc.). Following Cohen (1977),! treated" 0.5154 0.9317 0.2843 0.2953 0.2953 0.0110 0.0035 0.3445 0.0981)
  ("an r of .50 as a large exposure effect, whereas I considered an r" 0.5150 0.9297 0.2988 0.3098 0.3098 0.0110 0.0040 0.2871 0.0155)
  ("of .30 to be a moderate effect. An r of. 10 is considered a small" 0.5146 0.9287 0.3127 0.3237 0.3237 0.0110 0.0042 0.3558 0.0140)
  ("effect." 0.5150 0.5525 0.3266 0.3376 0.3376 0.0110 nil 0.1818 0.0375)
  ("Before assessing the influence of specific variables on the size" 0.5304 0.9288 0.3408 0.3518 0.3518 0.0110 0.0042 0.2705 0.0423)
  ("of the exposure effect, the relationship of critical variables" 0.5150 0.9294 0.3546 0.3656 0.3656 0.0110 0.0082 0.2438 0.0136)
  ("within experiments was examined to determine the distribu-" 0.5146 0.9290 0.3685 0.3795 0.3795 0.0110 0.0059 0.3266 0.0426)
  ("tion of different methodological and subject variables and to" 0.5146 0.9285 0.3827 0.3937 0.3937 0.0110 0.0068 0.2515 0.0257)
  ("rule out possible confounds due to systematic grouping of vari-" 0.5150 0.9290 0.3966 0.4076 0.4076 0.0110 0.0033 0.3303 0.0267)
  ("ables within studies. If a significant effect of a particular vari-" 0.5146 0.9286 0.4108 0.4218 0.4218 0.0110 0.0049 0.2804 0.0336)
  ("able on exposure effect size was found, but this variable was" 0.5146 0.9289 0.4250 0.4360 0.4360 0.0110 0.0056 0.2625 0.0277)
  ("unequally distributed across categories of another variable," 0.5146 0.9280 0.4389 0.4498 0.4498 0.0110 0.0084 0.2170 0.0655)
  ("then the observed effect of either variable might be due, in" 0.5142 0.9280 0.4527 0.4637 0.4637 0.0110 0.0072 0.2479 0.0289)
  ("whole or in part, to the effect of the other. Both chi-square anal-" 0.5150 0.9294 0.4669 0.4779 0.4779 0.0110 0.0037 0.3346 0.0392)
  ("yses of the distribution of pairs of methodological and subject" 0.5150 0.9287 0.4808 0.4918 0.4918 0.0110 0.0052 0.2631 0.0269)
  ("variables across categories of each other and perusal of fre-" 0.5154 0.9289 0.4950 0.5060 0.5060 0.0110 0.0073 0.1805 0.0588)
  ("quency tables summarizing the distribution of pairs of method-" 0.5146 0.9308 0.5092 0.5202 0.5202 0.0110 0.0037 0.3508 0.0476)
  ("ological and subject variables confirmed that variables included" 0.5146 0.9279 0.5234 0.5344 0.5344 0.0110 0.0030 0.3240 0.0502)
  ("in the meta-analysis were essentially \"randomly\" distributed" 0.5150 0.9283 0.5370 0.5479 0.5479 0.0110 0.0073 0.2963 0.0123)
  ("within studies." 0.5146 0.6108 0.5515 0.5624 0.5624 0.0110 0.0051 0.3378 0.0426)
  ("Results" 0.6950 0.7490 0.5836 0.5957 0.5957 0.0121 nil 0.3319 0.0540)
  ("Analysis of the size of the exposure effect for all 208 indepen-" 0.5300 0.9294 0.6073 0.6183 0.6183 0.0110 0.0035 0.3078 0.0564)
  ("dent experiments (collapsing across methodological and subject" 0.5142 0.9283 0.6215 0.6325 0.6325 0.0110 0.0036 0.3156 0.0289)
  ("variables) reveals that the combined effect size (r) for these stud-" 0.5150 0.9286 0.6354 0.6464 0.6464 0.0110 0.0032 0.3296 0.0640)
  ("ies is .260, with a combined z of 20.80 (p < .0000001) and a" 0.5146 0.9284 0.6492 0.6602 0.6602 0.0110 0.0048 0.3031 0.0183)
  ("fail-safe N of 33,047. Cohen (1977) considered an r of .260 to be" 0.5146 0.9284 0.6634 0.6744 0.6744 0.0110 0.0033 0.3410 0.0492)
  ("a moderate effect size. Nonetheless, inspection of the combined" 0.5146 0.9285 0.6773 0.6883 0.6883 0.0110 0.0037 0.3399 0.0076)
  ("effect size, combined z, and fail-safe N for all mere exposure" 0.5142 0.9284 0.6915 0.7025 0.7025 0.0110 0.0065 0.2872 0.0331)
  ("studies clearly indicates that the exposure-affect relationship is" 0.5146 0.9289 0.7054 0.7164 0.7164 0.0110 0.0036 0.2871 0.0452)
  ("robust and reliable." 0.5146 0.6425 0.7196 0.7306 0.7306 0.0110 0.0038 0.2694 0.0425)
  ("A stem-and-leaf diagram showing the distribution of the" 0.5296 0.9281 0.7335 0.7444 0.7444 0.0110 0.0080 0.3027 0.0116)
  ("combined effect size (r) for all 208 experiments examining the" 0.5146 0.9284 0.7477 0.7586 0.7586 0.0110 0.0044 0.3360 0.0658)
  ("exposure-affect relationship (collapsing across methodological" 0.5146 0.9283 0.7615 0.7725 0.7725 0.0110 0.0054 0.2580 0.1015)
  ("and subject variables) is presented in Figure 1. Numbers in the" 0.5150 0.9288 0.7757 0.7867 0.7867 0.0110 0.0046 0.3250 0.0237)
  ("left-hand column represent the tenths place of the correlation" 0.5146 0.9284 0.7896 0.8006 0.8006 0.0110 0.0050 0.2690 0.0580)
  ("coefficient, and numbers to the right of this column represent" 0.5146 0.9283 0.8035 0.8145 0.8145 0.0110 0.0048 0.3050 0.0699)
  ("the hundredths place. As Figure 1 shows, exposure effect sizes" 0.5146 0.9294 0.8174 0.8284 0.8284 0.0110 0.0039 0.2864 0.0197)
  ("range from -.81 to .93, with most coefficients clustered be-" 0.5146 0.9294 0.8312 0.8422 0.8422 0.0110 0.0073 0.3105 0.0359)
  ("tween .0 and .6. As this figure also shows, the distribution of" 0.5146 0.9287 0.8451 0.8561 0.8561 0.0110 0.0051 0.3018 0.0384)
  ("exposure effect sizes is reasonably symmetrical and normally" 0.5146 0.9289 0.8587 0.8697 0.8697 0.0110 0.0053 0.2954 0.0601)
  ("distributed." 0.5142 0.5904 0.8729 0.8839 0.8839 0.0110 nil 0.2611 0.0762)
  ("The results of the meta-analysis are presented in Tables 1-7." 0.5300 0.9292 0.8868 0.8978 0.8978 0.0110 0.0043 0.2920 0.0263))))
